    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d409">

                <pb facs="0669" n="655" sameAs="frus:frus1950v06/pg655"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">460.509/7–2450</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in France</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Gray</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the
                            Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Paris</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1950-07-24T16:00:00+01:00">July 24, 1950—4 p.
                            m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>429. Excon. ReDeptels 340, July 20, repeated
                    London 346; 395, July 21, repeated London 406.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d409-f1"/>
                </p>
                <list>
                    <label>1.</label>
                    <item>At joint US–UK request Cocom today recommended following petroleum
                        products for list I control at all Soviet-dominated destinations: <list>
                            <label>(I)</label>
                            <item>Crude petroleum.</item>
                            <label>(II)</label>
                            <item>Motor fuel.</item>
                            <label>(III)</label>
                            <item>Diesel oil (all grades).</item>
                            <label>(IV)</label>
                            <item>Fuel oil.</item>
                            <label>(V)</label>
                            <item>Kerosene.</item>
                            <label>(VI)</label>
                            <item>Lubricating oils and grease.</item>
                        </list>
                    </item>
                    <pb facs="0670" n="656" xml:id="pg656"/>
                    <label>2.</label>
                    <item>Above recommendation will become CoCom decision when delegations inform
                        chairman concurrence their respective governments. Concurrence all
                        governments within 48 hours has been requested.<anchor n="2"
                            xml:id="d409-f2"/>
                    </item>
                </list>
                <p>Sent Department 429, repeated info London 125.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gray</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d409-f1" n="1" xml:id="d409-f1-content">
                <p>Neither printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d409-f2" n="2" xml:id="d409-f2-content">
                <p>The concurrence of the CoCom governments was reported in telegram 513, July 28,
                    from Paris, which reads in part as follows:</p>
                <p>“1. Reference Embtel 420, July 24, repeated
                    London 125, CoCom delegates today reported concurrence their governments CoCom
                    recommendation petroleum products. Items listed in reference telegram now on
                    list I.</p>
                <p>“2. French stated their administrative arrangements not yet complete. Belgium
                    reminded committee list I exports to Yugoslavia not prohibited. Netherlands
                    reiterated statement made at July 17 meeting (Embtel 283, July 17) about differentiating North Korea and China
                    situations and need for cooperation Far Eastern non-PCs. Canadian said his
                    government believed embargo of petroleum products by PCs should be given
                    publicity.…” (460.500/7–2850)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
